!deck: Math::Calculus
!tags: Math Calculus Differentiation
!type: Basic Math

!id: ax8Frvkg1t
!front: State the Extreme Value Theorem (EVT).
!back:
The Extreme Value Theorem states that if a real-valued function \( f \) is:

- continuous on \( [a,b] \)
- \( [a,b] \) is closed and bounded

then \( f \) attains a maximum and a minimum on \( [a,b] \), each at least once: there exist \( c, d \in [a,b] \) such that
\[ f(c) \leq f(x) \leq f(d) \quad \forall x \in [a,b]. \]
!extra: Informally: a continuous function on a closed interval always has a highest point and a lowest point.
~~~

!id: RKhaYcqpZ1
!front: State Rolle's Theorem.
!back:
The Rolle's Theorem states that if a function \( f \) is:

- continuous on \( [a,b] \)
- differentiable on \( (a,b) \)
- \( f(a) = f(b) \)

then there exists at least one \( c \in (a,b) \) such that \( f'(c) = 0 \).
!extra:
Geometrically: if a continuous, differentiable curve starts and ends at the same height, some point in between has a horizontal tangent.

![Rolle's Theorem](img/rolle-theorem.svg)
~~~

!id: Nul59WMDrC
!front: State the Mean Value Theorem (MVT).
!back:
The Mean Value Theorem states that if a function \( f \) is:

- continuous on \( [a,b] \)
- differentiable on \( (a,b) \)

then there exists at least one \( c \in (a,b) \) such that the instantaneous rate of change at \( c \) equals the average rate of change over \( [a,b] \):
\[ f'(c) = \frac{f(b) - f(a)}{b - a}. \]
!extra:
Geometrically: there is at least one point on the curve where the tangent line is parallel to the secant line connecting the endpoints of the curve.

![Mean Value Theorem](img/mean-value-theorem.svg)
~~~

!id: n7ee7EmSmb
!front: What is the connection between the Rolle's Theorem and Mean Value Theorem?
!back:
The Rolle's Theorem is a special case of the Mean Value Theorem.

The Rolle's Theorem can be derived from the Mean Value Theorem: if \( f(a) = f(b) \), then the right side of the Mean Value Theorem equation becomes

\[ f'(c) = \frac{f(a) - f(a)}{b - a} = \frac{0}{b - a} = 0 \]
~~~

!id: 7sz7MRtXZk
!front: State the Intermediate Value Theorem (IVT).
!back:
The Intermediate Value Theorem states that if:

- \( f \) is continuous on \( [a,b] \)
- \( L \) is any value between \( f(a) \) and \( f(b) \)

then there exists at least one \( c \in [a,b] \) such that \( f(c) = L \).
!extra: Intuitively: if you can draw the graph without lifting your pencil, and it goes from one \( y \)-value to another, it must pass through every \( y \)-value in between.
~~~

!id: UAn7ziYq3m
!front: Construct a counterexample showing the IVT's conclusion can fail if \( f \) is not continuous on \( [a,b] \).
!back:
Let, for some \( m \in (a,b) \),
\[ f(x) = \begin{cases} -1 & a \le x < m \\ 1 & m \le x \le b \end{cases} \]

- \( f(a) = -1 \) and \( f(b) = 1 \), so \( 0 \) lies between them.
- \( f \) never takes the value \( 0 \) anywhere on \( [a,b] \).

The jump discontinuity at \( m \) lets \( f \) skip over \( 0 \) entirely.

![IVT counterexample](img/ivt-counterexample.svg)
!extra: Compare with the Intermediate Value Theorem, which requires continuity on \( [a,b] \) as a hypothesis.
~~~

!id: jBHWk5ISDd
!front: Construct a counterexample showing the EVT's conclusion can fail if \( f \) is not continuous on \( [a,b] \).
!back:
Let
\[ f(x) = \begin{cases} x & a \le x < b \\ a & x = b \end{cases} \]
on the closed bounded interval \( [a,b] \).

- As \( x \to b^- \), \( f(x) \) gets arbitrarily close to \( b \).
- \( f \) never equals \( b \), since \( f(b) = a \neq b \).

So \( f \) has no maximum on \( [a,b] \), even though the interval is closed and bounded.

![EVT counterexample](img/evt-counterexample.svg)
!extra: Compare with the Extreme Value Theorem, which requires continuity on \( [a,b] \) as a hypothesis.
~~~
